\title[Complementary products of monomial symmetric functions]
{Complementary Products of Monomial Symmetric Functions:\\
A Quadratic-Statistic Approach to Kleber's Splitting Phenomenon}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Kleber's rectangular-complement conjecture asserts that, for a fixed rectangle $\rho=(c^r)$, the
products $s_\lambda s_{\lambda^\vee}$ of a Schur function with the Schur function of its
rectangular complement, indexed by unordered complementary pairs, are linearly independent in the
ring of symmetric functions over an arbitrary commutative ring. The underlying phenomenon is a
\emph{componentwise splitting} statement: for every partition $\theta$, the products
$s_\alpha s_\beta$ are linearly independent as $\{\alpha,\beta\}$ ranges over unordered pairs with
$\alpha+\beta=\theta$. Both statements are invisible to dominance triangularity, since every
splitting of $\theta$ has the same dominance-leading term.

We give a complete and self-contained treatment of the analogous phenomenon for \emph{monomial}
symmetric functions, driven by a filtration transverse to dominance. The engine is the quadratic
statistic $Q(d)=\sum_i d_i^2$ on exponent vectors, satisfying
$Q(u+v)=Q(u)+Q(v)+2\langle u,v\rangle$; consequently the $Q$-minimal monomials of a product
$m_\alpha m_\beta$ are exactly those whose part multiset is the multiset union
$\alpha\uplus\beta$. Our main theorem: if a finite family of pairs $(\alpha_i,\beta_i)$ fits into
the ambient number of variables and the unions $\alpha_i\uplus\beta_i$ are pairwise distinct, then
the products $m_{\alpha_i}m_{\beta_i}$ are linearly independent over any integral domain of
characteristic zero, in particular over $\ZZ$ and over any field of characteristic zero. We derive
the componentwise-splitting and Kleber-shape specialisations, prove the one-row case
unconditionally, show that a product determines the multiset union of its factors, and extend the
mechanism to arbitrarily many factors, obtaining the linear independence of power-sum monomials.
We delimit the method sharply: the number-of-variables hypothesis is necessary, the distinct-union
hypothesis is not, and genuine collisions exist -- the smallest of which, arising from
$\theta=(5,3)$, is nevertheless resolved here, its two products being separated two merge layers
above the bottom of the filtration. Two conjectures, on union classes and on a merge
filtration, would together yield the full componentwise splitting theorem in the monomial basis.
\end{abstract}

